      "text": "To solve the given problem \\( 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 \\), we can utilize logarithms and their properties to simplify the calculation.\n\n1. First, let's calculate \\( 4^4 \\) and \\( 5^4 \\):\n   \\[\n   4^4 = (2^2)^4 = 2^{2 \\cdot 4} = 2^8 = 256\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5^4 = (5)^4\n   \\]\n\n2. Next, we note that \\( 5^4 \\) can be directly computed. However, if you prefer to keep it as a power of 5, we have:\n   \\[\n   5^4 = 5 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 5 = 625\n   \\]\n\n3. We now multiply these results:\n   \\[\n   4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = 256 \\cdot 625\n   \\]\n\n4. To multiply these numbers, we use the standard multiplication for integers. Start from the rightmost digits and move to the left:\n   - \\( 625 \\) has 3 digits, and \\( 256 \\) has 3 digits. Therefore we will write a 6-digit number after multiplication:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     625 \\\\\n     \\times 256 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n5. Compute using usual position of numbers:\n   - Multiply the units: \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\rightarrow\\) with 2 more ones \\( 625 \\) units gives 625, add them together over units, carry 3.\n   - Next digit is the same: multiply with units, carry the rest from the previous step.\n   - The carry in the previous step once out. 12, having 4, and then add the next digit in units place over four. This will be easy, just 12.\n   - On 6, similar calculations will be completed, not enumerating all digits, but the line of the fourth-to-similar step of us will be correct:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\mathrm{1250} \\\\\n     + \\mathrm{1024} \\\\\n     \\hline\n     \\mathrm{2780}\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n6. The outcome of the particular calculation is:\n   \\[\n   4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = 2^8 \\cdot 5^4 = 4096 \\cdot 625 = 2560000\n   \\]\n\nIn summary, we found that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2560000}\n\\]",
